@page "/register"
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@attribute [EnableRateLimiting(RateLimitedSurfaces.GuestRegistrationPolicy)]
@using MyRestaurant.Domain.Identifiers
@using MyRestaurant.Domain.Time
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.RateLimiting
@using MyRestaurant.WebApplication.Security
@inject IGuestRegistration Registration
@inject IPasswordHasher<Person> PasswordHasher
@inject SignInManager<Person> SignInManager
@inject UserManager<Person> UserManager
@inject IIdentifierFactory IdentifierFactory
@inject IClock Clock
@inject IDataProtectionProvider DataProtection
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject RestaurantTime RestaurantClock

@* Guest self-registration (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §4.3, §4.4, §11.1).

   §4.3: "Guests self-register at the moment of joining a table: username, optional display name, and
   at least one credential — passkey offered first, password accepted." §11.1 spells out the route in:
   "Anonymous with valid token → grant → sign-in/registration (passkey-first, password offered) → join."
   This is the registration half of that sentence; the join grant written before the detour (§4.4) is
   what survives it, which is the whole reason the grant exists.

   Anonymous and static SSR, like every account surface: it writes cookies on the response — the
   accumulating registration ticket, and the authentication cookie once the account exists — and a
   Blazor circuit cannot set either.

   TWO STEPS, driven by a Data-Protection-protected cookie (RegistrationTicket):

     1. Details — username, optional display name, optional password. The password is hashed
        immediately, so the ticket carries a PHC string and never a plaintext. The person's UUIDv7 is
        minted here because the WebAuthn ceremony in step 2 needs a user handle before the account
        exists, and it must equal the eventual person row's id (§3.3).

     2. Passkey — the real attestation ceremony against /register/passkey/creation-options, which is
        anonymous and gated on that same cookie. Registering commits the account; "Not now" commits it
        with the password alone, and appears ONLY when a password was set. That asymmetry is §3.3's
        rule made structural: a passkey is "always offered, never required, never a gate for guests",
        but an account with neither credential could never be signed into, so declining is offered
        exactly when there is something to fall back on.

   Nothing is written until whichever branch of step 2 the guest takes; the commit is one transaction
   (IGuestRegistration) covering the person, the passkey, and the audit rows. The new guest is signed
   in on the same response and sent to ReturnUrl — for the scan flow, back to /table/{id}, where the
   grant is waiting and the join button is one click away.

   No TOTP step, unlike /setup. §3.4 pairs the authenticator with the password path for staff and
   administrators; a guest is never required to hold one and may enroll voluntarily later from
   /account/enroll-totp. No role is granted either: a guest is the absence of a grant (§3.7).

   RATE LIMITED as of Slice 62 (§11.8, §13, §17, F-115), by the policy named in the attribute above.
   Read the budget as a volume ceiling, not as a brute-force defence: there is no secret to guess here,
   and F-37's ruling that the worst outcome of an unlimited /register is ROWS rather than access still
   stands. What matters for anybody editing this page is the partition — it is the client address, and
   over the tunnel from a restaurant's own wifi that is ONE address for the entire dining room. So the
   budget is sized for a full room rather than against an attacker, and it is configurable because the
   right number is a property of the room. Tightening it is not a free security improvement: it is
   traded against a seated guest who cannot create the account they need in order to order dinner. *@

<PageTitle>Create an account</PageTitle>

@if (_alreadySignedIn)
{
    <section class="panel account-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">MyRestaurant</p>
        <h1>You already have an account</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            You are signed in as <strong>@HttpContext.User.Identity?.Name</strong>, so there is nothing
            to create.
        </p>
        <div class="form-actions">
            <a class="button-primary" href="@SafeReturnUrl">Continue</a>
        </div>
    </section>
}
else
{
    <section class="panel account-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">MyRestaurant</p>

        <ol class="setup-steps" aria-label="Registration progress">
            <li class="@StepClass(0)">Your details</li>
            <li class="@StepClass(1)">Sign-in method</li>
        </ol>

        @if (_errorMessage is not null)
        {
            <p class="status-error" role="alert">@_errorMessage</p>
        }

        @if (_ticket is null)
        {
            @* Step 1 — who you are, and (optionally) a password. *@
            <h1>Create an account</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                Your table-mates see your display name next to what you order, so pick something they
                will recognise. Nothing else is required — no email address, no phone number.
            </p>

            <EditForm Model="DetailsInput" method="post" OnValidSubmit="StartAsync" FormName="register-details">
                <DataAnnotationsValidator />

                <div class="form-field">
                    <label for="username">Username</label>
                    <InputText id="username" @bind-Value="DetailsInput.Username" autocomplete="username"
                               autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false" autofocus />
                    <ValidationMessage For="@(() => DetailsInput.Username)" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-field">
                    <label for="display-name">Display name <span class="optional">(optional)</span></label>
                    <InputText id="display-name" @bind-Value="DetailsInput.DisplayName" autocomplete="name" />
                    <ValidationMessage For="@(() => DetailsInput.DisplayName)" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-field">
                    <label for="password">Password <span class="optional">(optional)</span></label>
                    <InputText id="password" type="password" @bind-Value="DetailsInput.Password"
                               autocomplete="new-password" />
                    <ValidationMessage For="@(() => DetailsInput.Password)" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-field">
                    <label for="confirm-password">Confirm password</label>
                    <InputText id="confirm-password" type="password" @bind-Value="DetailsInput.ConfirmPassword"
                               autocomplete="new-password" />
                    <ValidationMessage For="@(() => DetailsInput.ConfirmPassword)" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-actions">
                    <button type="submit" class="button-primary">Continue</button>
                </div>
            </EditForm>

            <p class="form-footnote">
                Leave the password blank to use a passkey only — your fingerprint, face, or device PIN,
                and nothing to remember. If you do set one, use at least
                @MinimumPasswordLength characters; your phone's password manager can
                generate and store a strong one.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            @* Step 2 — the credential itself. *@
            <h1>How will you sign in?</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                A passkey signs you in with your fingerprint, face, or device PIN. There is nothing to
                remember and nothing to type, and it cannot be phished. Add one now for
                <strong>@_ticket.Username</strong>.
            </p>

            <form class="passkey-add" @formname="register-passkey" method="post" @onsubmit="RegisterPasskeyAsync">
                <AntiforgeryToken />
                <PasskeySubmit Operation="PasskeyOperation.Create" Name="PasskeyInput"
                               CreationOptionsUrl="@AccountRoutes.RegistrationPasskeyCreationOptions"
                               class="button-primary">
                    Add a passkey
                </PasskeySubmit>
            </form>

            @if (_ticket.CanDeclineThePasskey)
            {
                <form @formname="register-skip-passkey" method="post" @onsubmit="FinishWithPasswordOnlyAsync">
                    <AntiforgeryToken />
                    <div class="form-actions">
                        <button type="submit" class="button-secondary">Not now — use my password</button>
                    </div>
                </form>

                <p class="form-footnote">
                    You can add a passkey later from your account page, and keep the password as a
                    backup.
                </p>
            }
            else
            {
                <p class="form-footnote">
                    You did not set a password, so a passkey is how you will get back in. Prefer a
                    password? <strong>Start over</strong> below and fill that field in.
                </p>
            }

            <form @formname="register-restart" method="post" @onsubmit="RestartAsync" class="setup-restart">
                <AntiforgeryToken />
                <button type="submit" class="link-button">Start over</button>
            </form>
        }

        <p class="form-footnote">
            Already have an account? <a href="@SignInUrl">Sign in</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The §3.2 password floor, mirrored from <c>AddRestaurantIdentity</c>'s
    /// <c>identity.Password.RequiredLength</c>. Stated here rather than read from
    /// <c>IdentityOptions</c> for the same reason <c>Setup.razor</c> states it: the number appears in
    /// prose a guest reads as well as in a rule, and a page that said "at least 12 characters" while
    /// validating against something else would be worse than one that repeats the literal.
    /// </summary>
    private const int MinimumPasswordLength = 12;

    private RegistrationTicketProtector _protector = default!;
    private RegistrationTicket? _ticket;
    private bool _alreadySignedIn;
    private string? _errorMessage;

    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext HttpContext { get; set; } = default!;

    [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "register-details")]
    private DetailsInputModel DetailsInput { get; set; } = default!;

    [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "register-passkey")]
    private PasskeyInputModel PasskeyInput { get; set; } = default!;

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    private string? ReturnUrl { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the guest goes once the account exists — for the scan flow, back to the table.</summary>
    private string SafeReturnUrl => ObligationsEnforcement.SafeLocalReturnUrl(ReturnUrl);

    /// <summary>This page's own URL, carrying the return URL across the step-to-step redirects.</summary>
    private string SelfUrl => string.IsNullOrEmpty(ReturnUrl)
        ? AccountRoutes.Register
        : $"{AccountRoutes.Register}?ReturnUrl={Uri.EscapeDataString(ReturnUrl)}";

    /// <summary>The sign-in page, carrying the same return URL so the two surfaces are interchangeable.</summary>
    private string SignInUrl => string.IsNullOrEmpty(ReturnUrl)
        ? AccountRoutes.SignIn
        : $"{AccountRoutes.SignIn}?ReturnUrl={Uri.EscapeDataString(ReturnUrl)}";

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // BL0008: a form POST binds these itself and may assign null, so the defaults live here.
        DetailsInput ??= new();
        PasskeyInput ??= new();
        _protector = new RegistrationTicketProtector(DataProtection);

        if (HttpContext.User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true)
        {
            _alreadySignedIn = true;
            return;
        }

        if (HttpContext.Request.Cookies.TryGetValue(RegistrationCookie.Name, out string? cookie)
            && _protector.TryUnprotect(cookie, out RegistrationTicket? ticket)
            && ticket is not null
            && !ticket.HasExpired(Clock.UtcNow, RegistrationCookie.Lifetime))
        {
            _ticket = ticket;
        }
        else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(cookie))
        {
            // Stale, tampered, or from another key ring: drop it and start clean.
            RegistrationCookie.Delete(HttpContext.Response);
            _errorMessage = "That took a while, so we started over. Please fill this in again.";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Step 1's commit: mint the identity, hash any password, and hand the guest to the credential
    /// step. Writes nothing to the database — the username check here is advisory, and the UNIQUE
    /// constraint inside the final transaction is what actually decides (§4.3).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartAsync()
    {
        if (_alreadySignedIn)
        {
            return;
        }

        string username = DetailsInput.Username.Trim();
        string? displayName = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(DetailsInput.DisplayName)
            ? null
            : DetailsInput.DisplayName.Trim();

        if (await UserManager.FindByNameAsync(username) is not null)
        {
            _errorMessage = "That username is already taken. Please choose another.";
            return;
        }

        Guid personIdentifier = IdentifierFactory.Create();

        // Hashed here so the plaintext never enters the ticket — the same discipline /setup uses. The
        // hasher ignores its user argument (no per-user pepper, §3.2), so a throwaway carrier is fine.
        string? passwordHash = string.IsNullOrEmpty(DetailsInput.Password)
            ? null
            : PasswordHasher.HashPassword(
                new Person { PersonIdentifier = personIdentifier, Username = username },
                DetailsInput.Password);

        WriteTicketAndReload(new RegistrationTicket(
            personIdentifier,
            Clock.UtcNow,
            username,
            displayName,
            passwordHash));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Step 2, passkey branch: verify the attestation the browser produced, then commit the account
    /// with it (§3.3, §4.3).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task RegisterPasskeyAsync()
    {
        if (_alreadySignedIn)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (_ticket is null)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(SelfUrl);
            return;
        }

        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(PasskeyInput.Error))
        {
            _errorMessage = "That passkey could not be added. Please try again.";
            return;
        }

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(PasskeyInput.CredentialJson))
        {
            _errorMessage = "Your browser did not provide a passkey.";
            return;
        }

        PasskeyAttestationResult attestation =
            await SignInManager.PerformPasskeyAttestationAsync(PasskeyInput.CredentialJson);
        if (!attestation.Succeeded || attestation.Passkey is null)
        {
            _errorMessage = "That passkey could not be registered. Please try again.";
            return;
        }

        UserPasskeyInfo verified = attestation.Passkey;

        // Rebuilt rather than passed through, for the same reason /setup rebuilds it: created_at is
        // stamped by the data layer so every row in the transaction shares one instant, and the
        // attestation object and client-data JSON are deliberately not persisted (attestation is
        // 'none', §3.3).
        UserPasskeyInfo passkey = new(
            verified.CredentialId,
            verified.PublicKey,
            Clock.UtcNow,
            verified.SignCount,
            verified.Transports,
            verified.IsUserVerified,
            verified.IsBackupEligible,
            verified.IsBackedUp,
            attestationObject: [],
            clientDataJson: [])
        {
            Name = $"Passkey added {RestaurantClock.Date(Clock.UtcNow)}",
        };

        await CommitAsync(_ticket, passkey);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Step 2, declined branch: commit the account on the password alone. Reachable only when a
    /// password was set — the button is not rendered otherwise, and this re-checks rather than trusting
    /// the render that drew it, because a POST is a separate request.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task FinishWithPasswordOnlyAsync()
    {
        if (_alreadySignedIn)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (_ticket is null)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(SelfUrl);
            return;
        }

        if (!_ticket.CanDeclineThePasskey)
        {
            _errorMessage = "You have not set a password, so you will need a passkey to sign in.";
            return;
        }

        await CommitAsync(_ticket, passkey: null);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one write. Creates the account in a single transaction, clears the ticket, signs the new
    /// guest in on this response, and continues to wherever they were going (§4.4 — for the scan flow
    /// that is <c>/table/{id}</c>, where the join grant is still waiting).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task CommitAsync(RegistrationTicket ticket, UserPasskeyInfo? passkey)
    {
        GuestRegistrationStatus status = await Registration.RegisterAsync(
            new NewGuestAccount(
                ticket.PersonIdentifier,
                ticket.Username,
                ticket.DisplayName,
                ticket.PasswordHash,
                passkey),
            HttpContext.RequestAborted);

        if (status == GuestRegistrationStatus.UsernameTaken)
        {
            // Somebody took the name while this guest was choosing a credential. The passkey they may
            // have just created is bound to a user handle no account will ever carry, so the honest
            // thing is to start over rather than pretend the ticket is still usable.
            RegistrationCookie.Delete(HttpContext.Response);
            _ticket = null;
            _errorMessage = "That username was taken while you were signing up. Please try another.";
            return;
        }

        RegistrationCookie.Delete(HttpContext.Response);

        Person? guest = await UserManager.FindByIdAsync(ticket.PersonIdentifier.ToString());
        if (guest is not null)
        {
            // isPersistent per §3.1: the 24-hour sliding session should survive a phone locking itself
            // mid-meal. No security_event is written here — RestaurantSignInManager audits the sign-in
            // *paths*, and account_created is already on the record from the transaction above.
            await SignInManager.SignInAsync(guest, isPersistent: true);
        }

        Navigation.NavigateTo(SafeReturnUrl);
    }

    private void RestartAsync()
    {
        RegistrationCookie.Delete(HttpContext.Response);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(SelfUrl);
    }

    // Writes the protected ticket cookie and follows post/redirect/get so a refresh does not re-post
    // the step. NavigateTo during static SSR surfaces as an HTTP redirect carrying the fresh cookie.
    private void WriteTicketAndReload(RegistrationTicket ticket)
    {
        RegistrationCookie.Write(HttpContext.Response, _protector.Protect(ticket));
        Navigation.NavigateTo(SelfUrl);
    }

    private int ActiveStepIndex => _ticket is null ? 0 : 1;

    private string StepClass(int index)
        => index == ActiveStepIndex ? "setup-step is-current"
            : index < ActiveStepIndex ? "setup-step is-done"
            : "setup-step";

    private sealed class DetailsInputModel : IValidatableObject
    {
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Choose a username.")]
        [StringLength(64, MinimumLength = 3, ErrorMessage = "The username must be 3–64 characters.")]
        public string Username { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        [StringLength(120, ErrorMessage = "That display name is too long.")]
        public string? DisplayName { get; set; }

        // Deliberately not [Required]: a passkey-only account is the passkey-first default (§4.3).
        // The length FLOOR is enforced in Validate below rather than by StringLength's MinimumLength,
        // which would also fire on the empty string and make "no password" impossible to express. The
        // ceiling is an attribute because it has no such exception — and because Argon2id on an
        // unbounded input is work an anonymous caller should not be able to ask for (§3.2).
        [StringLength(256, ErrorMessage = "That password is too long.")]
        public string? Password { get; set; }

        public string? ConfirmPassword { get; set; }

        /// <summary>
        /// The password rules that only apply once there is a password: it meets the §3.2 floor, and
        /// the confirmation matches. <c>[Compare]</c> is not used for the latter because it treats two
        /// nulls as a match but reports a bare field-level message that reads oddly on an optional
        /// field.
        /// </summary>
        public IEnumerable<ValidationResult> Validate(ValidationContext validationContext)
        {
            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(Password))
            {
                if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(ConfirmPassword))
                {
                    yield return new ValidationResult(
                        "Enter the password in both fields, or leave both blank to use a passkey.",
                        [nameof(Password)]);
                }

                yield break;
            }

            if (Password.Length < MinimumPasswordLength)
            {
                yield return new ValidationResult(
                    $"Use at least {MinimumPasswordLength} characters, or leave it blank to use a passkey.",
                    [nameof(Password)]);
            }

            if (!string.Equals(Password, ConfirmPassword, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                yield return new ValidationResult("The passwords do not match.", [nameof(ConfirmPassword)]);
            }
        }
    }
}
